WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:08.020
Justin Warren: Okay, next question.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:15.070
Film Courage: Okay and this is from Iglp3.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:19.250
Here is my question, my name is Chloe.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:23.439
How do you stay the course in spite of the
inner negative narrative so many writers deal

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:26.519
with that says “everything you make sucks?”

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:29.400
Justin: Man…that’s a good question…Oh!

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.599
That’s rich, that’s really rich.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:32.599
Oh man!

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:33.599
Film Courage: You’ve got us Chloe.

00:00:33.599 --> 00:00:34.820
Justin: Wow!

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.340
Yeah, I feel like I can speak on this for
days.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:51.020
I would say in particular…I don’t want
to get too dark here but I think writing is

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.520
inherently like the only person you are battling
is yourself really (most of the time).

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.890
And I think in particular at least for me
I can only speak for my own experience, I

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.930
struggle a lot with self-hatred.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.560
There are lots of parts of myself that I hate.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.991
And I am only just now sort of getting over
those types of things but when it’s just

00:01:12.991 --> 00:01:20.220
you and a blank screen and a cursor flashing
sometimes all of your insecurities and bubble

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:27.740
up and it gets in the way of you getting your
creative thoughts on the page.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.640
And I’ve learned just for me like when I’m
able to give myself permission to write the

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:40.350
worst, most terrible pages of all time then
I can do it.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:45.740
And then in my back pocket I know that writing
is process.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:51.040
So I know that whatever I try on my first
attempt is probably not going to be very good.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:59.010
In fact the terrible first draft, that’s
just kind of a law in how I see it.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:03.580
There are other people who are brilliant that
can pump out a genius first draft, I’m not

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.040
that person, I will never be that person.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:15.380
And so for me when I’m allowed to give myself
grace and allow myself to write terribly,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.540
to just write things that don’t make sense,
to write sentence fragments…

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.480
“And then they fight…I’ll come back
later.”

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.560
And then keep going, you know what I mean?

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.650
Like when I allow myself that level of freedom,
then I can write.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:39.620
I feel like on paper I shouldn’t be a writer
because my self-criticism in my brain is pretty

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.540
fierce and sometimes it’s unbearable.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.819
But I think you do, you have to give yourself
grace.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:52.040
You have to…I don’t want to be too new-agey
but you’ve got to kind of love yourself.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.880
I mean you have to be okay with “Yep, these
are some terrible words, but you know they

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.680
are words and that’s all that matters right
now.”

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.040
In this particular instance, the only thing
that matters is putting words on the page,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.510
they don’t have to be the right words, they
don’t have to be in the right order.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:16.709
And at least for a first draft that’s the
thing that when I learned that sort of unlocked

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:17.709
in my brain.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:19.959
It’s like “Oh, this draft doesn’t count.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:25.510
It doesn’t count because I can go back and
fix it later.”

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.459
And so writing with a reckless abandon.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:30.900
Like this doesn’t have to be good.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.319
This is supposed to be bad.

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:38.099
Anyone can write a terrible thing, you know
what I mean (terrible just in terms of the

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:39.560
mechanics of words).

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:46.520
Anyone can write something terrible and that’s
how I find my entry into it.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.740
I know for a fact that I can write something
terrible.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.910
If my goal is to sit down and be a genius,
I can’t hit that every day.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.800
But I can hit being really bad every day.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:02.840
And so that’s my only metric of success
are there words on the page?

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:03.989
Yes.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:07.690
My metric of success is not are they good
words, is this good?

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:08.690
That’s for later.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:13.360
Film Courage: I’ve probably mentioned this
before but there is this thing called the

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.220
Dunning-Kruger effect and I’m probably butchering
the name of it but I think it’s something

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.940
to the effect of those who think they are
really horrible at something are actually

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.590
better than most people.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.030
I’m not sure I may be butchering this concept
but then those who think that “I’m really

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:37.090
good at this” they are actually mistaken
and it’s this weird duality.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.770
So people who are more (I don’t know if
the word is self-effacing) they are actually

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.130
better than what they think they are doing.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:50.310
So I don’t know, that’s just something
that came to mind.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:55.669
Justin: That kind of makes sense, I think
there might be something to that because I

00:04:55.669 --> 00:05:04.500
think you have to be relatively self-aware
(well, I guess you don’t have to be).

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:13.530
But I think self-awareness as a writer is
a good thing.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.780
But self-awareness gone unchecked can be unproductive.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:26.210
But if you can find a mechanism in a way that
you can use that as a tool as a way to look

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.759
at something critically and look at it later
critically, that’s a really good skill to

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:35.180
have because it allows you to empathize with
people which is really all that we’re trying

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.190
to do in our stories is create empathy between
the audience and the characters, that’s

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.759
all we’re trying to do.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:50.020
And I think people that might think “I’m
terrible at this.”

00:05:50.020 --> 00:06:00.310
I know I’m in that crowd, I think that can
be a strength when used like a tool used in

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.470
a very calculated way like “Okay, I wrote
this thing.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.759
It’s really bad.

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.400
I know it’s bad.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.590
How can I make it good?”

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:21.550
In that sort of not assuming that you have
all the answers I think can open you up to

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.750
making much more work long term.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.750
Film Courage: It reminds me of the two characters
in ADAPTATION?

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:27.789
Justin: Yeah!

00:06:27.789 --> 00:06:29.350
Love that movie.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:34.919
Film Courage: So you see the one character
[Charlie Kaufman] played by Nicholas Cage

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:41.050
is insecure and you know there is so much
he can do but it’s just a matter of what

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.229
he feels about himself.

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:43.820
And the other guy [Donald Kaufman, also played
by Nicholas Cage]…

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:48.039
Justin: Is just pumping it out and it like
“I think I sold this already?”

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:53.350
Film Courage: Women on both arms, nice cars,
nice things and it doesn’t make sense but

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:57.090
you see this sometimes in life and you wonder
how is that possible?

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:00.470
Well I think it’s just someone’s perception
of themselves.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.389
It’s this weird thing.

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:05.240
Justin: Absolutely.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:15.350
Film Courage: Because if you think you’re
terrible you are probably not going to get

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.090
the words out.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:24.000
And so when I feel like for me I shouldn’t
even be a writer because I struggle so much

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.169
with self-hatred.

00:07:26.169 --> 00:07:33.910
But I learned if you just give yourself permission
to be terrible, you can do it because everybody

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:39.169
can be really bad at something.

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:43.520
I think there’s a John Lennon quote where
he’s like “I’m a musician, if you hand

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.570
me a tuba, I’ll get something out of it.”

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:48.889
And I’m like “I get that now."

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:55.889
He didn’t say it would be good, he just
said I could get something out of this.

00:07:55.889 --> 00:08:00.460
And I think if we create from that perspective,
especially with first drafts and first attempts

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:05.960
with things, you really can’t go wrong because
anybody can probably get something out of

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:06.960
a tuba.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.870
Film Courage: It might hurt your ears at first,
but then it would get better.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:15.419
Justin: It could shatter all the glass in
the room, but that’s okay.

